    "lawCode": "Add §2509, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1819\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to maternal mortality\nreview boards and the maternal mortality and morbidity advisory council\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend the Public Health Law, in relation to establishing the Maternal\nMortality Review Board for the purposes of reviewing maternal mortality\nand morbidity. The Board shall assess the cause of death and factors\nleading, to death and preventability for each maternal death reviewed,\nand to develop strategies for reducing the risk of maternal mortality.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill establishes the legislative findings and intent\nof the bill.\n\nSection two of the bill amends the Public Health Law by adding a new\nsection 2509, which establishes the Maternal Mortality Review Board for\npurposes of assessing the cause of death and factors leading to death\nand preventability for each maternal death reviewed, and to develop\nstrategies for reducing the risk of maternal mortality. It authorizes\nthe commissioner to enter into an agreement with the City of New York,\nincludes definitions of various terms, establishes the number of members\ncomprising the board, as well as the requirements for a quorum of the\nboard, and establishes a diversity requirement for members of the hoard.\nIt authorizes the commissioner and the city commissioner to receive\ninformation that will help the department carry out its functions. It\nestablishes that each board shall make and report findings to the\ncommissioner and city commissioner, and that the commissioner, city\ncommissioner, and the state and city boards shall each keep any informa-\ntion collected or received under this section that includes personal\nidentifying information of the woman, health care practitioner or anyone\nelse individually named in such information confidential. It also\nprovides that the commissioner and the city commissioner may us e the\nrecommendations and findings of the boards to develop guidance and other\nactions relating to best practices, and establishes an Advisory Council\nthat may review the findings of the boards, develop recommendations on\npolicies, best practices, and strategies to prevent maternal mortality\nand morbidity.\n\nSection three of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York currently ranks 30th out of 50 states in its maternal death\nrate. The issue is compounded by significant racial and ethnic dispari-\nties, with black women nearly four times more likely to die during preg-\nnancy and childbirth compared to white women. Moreover, maternal morbid-\nity and the severe life-threatening complications of delivery are even\nmore pervasive. The state must invest in mothers through creation of a\nmaternal mortality review board to create new strategies for the\nprevention of maternal mortality and morbidity.\n\n\nDevelopment of the maternal mortality review board will provide a deeper\nunderstanding of the causes and circumstances surrounding each maternal\ndeath. Through the convening of a multidisciplinary and diverse group of\nclinical experts, the maternal mortality review board will facilitate a\ncomprehensive, confidential review of maternal deaths and develop ò\nactionable recommendations for the prevention of future deaths.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
